      'v2.print.settings.petg': 'layer 0.16 mm, 4–5 walls, outer wall 60 mm/s, inner wall 100 mm/s at 5000 mm/s², no supports',
      'v2.print.settings.arc': 'layer 0.16 mm, 2 or more walls, no supports',
      'v2.print.settings.tpu': 'layer 0.16 mm, 100% infill (zig-zag), no supports',
      'v2.print.note.body': 'turn it 180° in the slicer',
      'v2.print.note.tray': 'turn it 180° in the slicer',
      'v2.print.how.title': 'How to print',
      'v2.print.how.orient': 'Every file already lies in the right position: the top is printed face down, the button cap upside down, the arc on its flat upper side, the plugs face down.',
      'v2.print.how.seam': 'Set the seam to the back (“Back”). In the slicer the body and the tray load with their front (the microphone and antenna side) toward the back of the bed, so turn both 180° before slicing: the seam then lands at the back of the speaker, on the button and USB-C side. The top already loads the right way round.',
      'v2.print.how.supports': 'No supports anywhere. The short bridges are meant to be there: over the USB-C opening of the tray, over the foot niches, on the ledges of the plug seats and in the screw-head recesses of the top. To keep them from sagging into strings, turn on “Thick bridges” in Bambu Studio (Quality → Bridging) and dry the PETG before printing: 65 °C for 6–8 hours.',
      'v2.print.how.layer': 'Print the body and the arc with a 0.16 mm layer: the height of the arc (2.88 mm) is fitted to the body bridge printed at that layer. The arc comes out 0.04 mm taller than the bridge, so the top presses on it lightly and it does not rattle. For another layer the height has to be recalculated in the model and the files built again.',
      'v2.print.how.walls': 'Arachne is the wall generator mode in Bambu Studio; the standard profile uses Classic, so turn on Arachne for every part.',
      'v2.print.how.tpu': 'Print the TPU parts with a 0.16 mm layer and 100% infill (zig-zag pattern), without supports.',
      'v2.solder.intro': 'Everything is soldered on the table before assembly: the microphones, the amplifier with its capacitor, the ring filament with the transistor, the button and the speaker driver, and then all the wires on the DevKitC. Each wire in the drawings is labelled with where it goes and its length: cut the wire to exactly that length, bared ends included (about 3 mm at each end). The lengths come from the case model and include some spare. Tip: if you first snap the DevKitC and the amplifier into the tray and glue the transistor in (the Tray stage of the Assembly section, steps 1–3), soldering is easier, because the tray holds them still.',
      'v2.solder.pinmap.title': 'DevKitC pin map',
      'v2.solder.pinmap.intro': 'The board lies in the tray labels up, antenna to the front and USB-C ports to the back. The front of the speaker is the side with the microphones and the antenna, the back is the side with the USB-C port, and left and right are as seen from behind. Row 1 is the row on the COM port side (right), row 2 the row on the side of the other USB port (left). Pins are counted from the antenna end, 1 to 22. Every wire is soldered to the tip of a long pin, 4 mm above the board.',
      'v2.solder.pinmap.caption': 'The pins in use: row, number from the antenna, the label on the board, what the pin does and which wires go to it.',
